# {{ name }}

A local development workspace for agents and MCP servers built on
`ai-agent-lib`. Owner: {{ owner }}.

## Start

```bash
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli install     # one environment for every service in the workspace
python -m pytest              # every test, with no network and no accounts
```

## Add services

```bash
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli new mcp hello-mcp        # an MCP server with a sample data source
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli new agent hello-agent    # a LangGraph agent
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli link hello-agent hello   # let the agent call the server's tools
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli install                   # install what was added
python -m pytest
```

For a server over your own data instead of the sample, give `new mcp` one of
`--from-csv <folder>`, `--from-openapi <file>` or `--from-redshift <schema>`.
Add `--propose` to see the tools it would write first.

Each command writes working code with its tests, registers the service and
adds the rules it needs. From there the code is yours: change a test first,
watch it fail, then change the code.

## Check and run

```bash
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli doctor                    # is everything each service needs usable?
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli config explain <service>  # every setting and where its value came from
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli run <service>             # start an agent's HTTP entry point or an MCP server
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli graph <agent>             # the agent's graph, as a Mermaid diagram
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli policy test               # do the rules decide the samples as expected?
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli update                    # take template changes from a newer agentlib
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli check                     # what CI runs: lint, format, types, tests, rules
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli eval [<service>]          # the evals, with the real model in each .env
python -m ai_agent_lib_cli deploy <service> --plan   # what running it on AWS takes, and why
```

`doctor` names what is wrong and how to fix it. Run it first when a service
does not start.

## What is here

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `agents/` | One folder per agent |
| `mcp-servers/` | One folder per MCP server |
| `registry/agents.yaml` | Which agents exist and which MCP servers each may call |
| `registry/mcp-tools.yaml` | Which MCP servers and tools exist, with a pin of each tool's input schema |
| `policies/agentlib/rules/data.yaml` | What every service allows. A request no rule matches is denied |
| `tests/` | Tests of the workspace as a whole, and of agents together with servers |
| `tests/policy-samples.yaml` | Requests the rules must allow or deny. Add one before you add a rule |
| `.github/workflows/check.yml` | The gate CI runs on every change, on Linux and Windows: `agentlib check` |
| `deploy/` | After `agentlib deploy`: Terraform and Dockerfiles for each deployed service |
| `agentlib.toml` | The answers this workspace was generated from |
| `agentlib.lock` | What each generated file held when it was written. Commit it: `agentlib update` uses it to leave your changes alone |

Every service reads the same registry and rules files, so an agent and the
servers it calls agree on who may do what.

## How a service is configured

Each service has a `.env.example`, which is committed and which its tests
read, and a `.env`, which is yours and is not committed. A path in either is
relative to the file, so a service finds its files whatever folder it is
started from. Local state (the audit log, conversation checkpoints) is written
to `.agentlib/` in the service's folder.

Nothing here needs an account or a network connection until you select a real
model or deploy.
